---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.7 — Informal Run-Time Analysis &amp; The RuntimeAnalyzer Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.12 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> On the AP Computer Science A exam,
    informal run-time analysis asks you to compare algorithms not with a stopwatch, but by counting
    <strong>how many times a key statement executes</strong> as the problem size <code>n</code>
    grows. Today you will build <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code> to instrument linear, logarithmic,
    and quadratic loop structures and prove how their step counts scale.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Spec-First Workflow (Download Both Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/RuntimeAnalyzer.java" download>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</a> — starter stub
            where you will build all four step-counting methods.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java" download>RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java</a> —
            final acceptance verification suite (12 checks across 3 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong> Trace the step counts by hand for small <code>n</code>
        values first, draft each method's pieces in the prompts below, assemble them in
        <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code>, and check your scaling predictions in a scratch
        <code>main</code> <em>before</em> running <code>RuntimeAnalyzerCheck</code> at the end.
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac RuntimeAnalyzer.java RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java
java RuntimeAnalyzerCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 1 &amp; 2 · Linear vs. Halving Search</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countLinearSearchSteps(int n, int targetIndex)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Simulate a linear search over indices <code>0</code> through <code>n - 1</code> looking for
        <code>targetIndex</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Loop <code>i</code> from <code>0</code> to <code>n - 1</code>.</li>
        <li>On every iteration, increment a <code>steps</code> counter by <code>1</code> (counting
            one comparison).</li>
        <li>If <code>i == targetIndex</code>, immediately return <code>steps</code> (found!).</li>
        <li>If the loop finishes without finding <code>targetIndex</code>, return
            <code>steps</code> (which equals <code>n</code> when <code>n &gt;= 0</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Best case: <code>(1000, 0) &rarr; 1</code> step.</li>
                <li>Middle case: <code>(1000, 499) &rarr; 500</code> steps.</li>
                <li>Worst case found: <code>(1000, 999) &rarr; 1000</code> steps.</li>
                <li>Not found: <code>(1000, -1) &rarr; 1000</code> and <code>(1000, 1500) &rarr; 1000</code> steps.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>countLinearSearchSteps</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Loop, Counter Placement &amp; Early Return):</strong> Write the
                <code>steps</code> initialization, <code>for</code> loop over <code>0 .. n - 1</code>,
                and the body — why must <code>steps++</code> execute <em>before</em>
                <code>if (i == targetIndex) return steps;</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Assemble <code>countLinearSearchSteps</code> in <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>countBinarySearchSteps(int n, int targetIndex)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Now instrument binary search (halving the remaining index range every step) over the sorted
        range <code>lo = 0</code> through <code>hi = n - 1</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Start with <code>lo = 0</code>, <code>hi = n - 1</code>, and <code>steps = 0</code>.</li>
        <li>While the active range is non-empty (<code>lo &lt;= hi</code>):
            <ol>
                <li>Increment <code>steps</code> by <code>1</code>.</li>
                <li>Compute the midpoint index <code>int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;</code> (integer division!).</li>
                <li>If <code>mid == targetIndex</code>, return <code>steps</code> immediately.</li>
                <li>Else if <code>mid &lt; targetIndex</code>, discard the left half by setting
                    <code>lo = mid + 1;</code>.</li>
                <li>Else (<code>mid &gt; targetIndex</code>), discard the right half by setting
                    <code>hi = mid - 1;</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
        <li>If the loop exits without finding <code>targetIndex</code> (<code>lo &gt; hi</code>),
            return <code>steps</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked traces:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>(1000, 499)</code> &rarr; first <code>mid = (0 + 999) / 2 = 499</code> &rarr; <code>1</code> step!</li>
                <li><code>(15, 14)</code> &rarr; <code>mid</code> visits <code>7 &rarr; 11 &rarr; 13 &rarr; 14</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> steps.</li>
                <li><code>(15, 99)</code> (not found) &rarr; <code>mid</code> visits <code>7 &rarr; 11 &rarr; 13 &rarr; 14</code>, then <code>lo = 15 &gt; hi = 14</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> steps.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: Logarithmic Scaling &amp; Avoiding Infinite Binary Search</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>When <code>n = 1023</code> and <code>targetIndex = 1022</code> (the very last
                element!), how many steps does <code>countLinearSearchSteps(1023, 1022)</code> take
                vs. <code>countBinarySearchSteps(1023, 1022)</code>? Why does doubling
                <code>n</code> from <code>1023</code> to <code>2047</code> add <code>1024</code>
                steps to linear search, but only <strong><code>1</code></strong> step to binary
                search?</li>
            <li>What happens on <code>countBinarySearchSteps(15, 99)</code> (when
                <code>lo == 14, hi == 14, mid == 14</code>) if a programmer writes
                <code>lo = mid;</code> instead of <code>lo = mid + 1;</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>countBinarySearchSteps</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Bounds &amp; Halving Loop):</strong> Write the setup variables
                (<code>lo</code>, <code>hi</code>, <code>steps</code>), the <code>while</code>
                header, the <code>steps++</code> and <code>mid</code> calculation, and the 3-way
                <code>if / else if / else</code> update + fallback <code>return</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Assemble <code>countBinarySearchSteps</code> in <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code> and
            hand-trace <code>(15, 14)</code> and <code>(15, 99)</code> to confirm both take
            <code>4</code> steps.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Quadratic Growth &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>countFullGridSteps(int n)</code> &amp; <code>countUniquePairSteps(int n)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Implement both nested-loop step counters (return <code>0</code> if <code>n &lt;= 0</code>):
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>countFullGridSteps(int n)</code> — counts how many times the inner body runs in a
            full <code>n &times; n</code> nested loop:
            <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++)</code>.</li>
        <li><code>countUniquePairSteps(int n)</code> — counts how many times the inner body runs
            when comparing only unique pairs:
            <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) for (int j = i + 1; j &lt; n; j++)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>countFullGridSteps(10) &rarr; 100</code>, <code>countFullGridSteps(0) &rarr; 0</code>.</li>
                <li><code>countUniquePairSteps(10) &rarr; 45</code> (<code>10 * 9 / 2</code>), <code>countUniquePairSteps(1) &rarr; 0</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for Part 3</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Instrument Both Loops &amp; Boundary Check):</strong> Write the
                <code>steps</code> counters inside both nested loop shapes, and verify why
                <code>countUniquePairSteps(1)</code> returns <code>0</code> while
                <code>countFullGridSteps(1)</code> returns <code>1</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Assemble both methods in <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code>, then print their outputs for
            <code>n = 10</code>, <code>n = 100</code>, and <code>n = 1000</code> in your own
            <code>main</code> before answering the scaling question below.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: Does Starting at <code>j = i + 1</code> Escape Quadratic Growth?</h3>
        <p>
            Many students assume that because <code>j = i + 1</code> skips roughly half the grid,
            it is no longer quadratic. Check the numbers from your own methods below.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>Compare <code>countUniquePairSteps(10)</code> (<code>45</code>),
                <code>countUniquePairSteps(100)</code> (<code>4,950</code>), and
                <code>countUniquePairSteps(1000)</code> (<code>499,500</code>). When <code>n</code>
                grows by a factor of <strong>10&times;</strong> (from 100 to 1,000), by roughly what
                factor does the number of steps grow (10&times; or 100&times;)? Why does cutting a
                quadratic algorithm in half still leave it quadratic at scale?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods in <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code> were built from your pieces and self-tested before running the check</li>
            <li><code>java RuntimeAnalyzerCheck</code> reports <strong><code>12 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection and code-piece boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/run-time-analysis.html">Run-Time Analysis</a> — linear, quadratic, and logarithmic step counting</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/loop-analysis.html">Tracing Loops</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
